From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vipul Cariappa <vipulcariappa@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 08:27:55 +0530
Subject: Build libcppjit as a wasm side module

cppjit's build assumed a host toolchain in three places that a cross build to
emscripten cannot satisfy.

Python: find_package(Python COMPONENTS Development) resolves the *host*
interpreter, whose headers are the wrong architecture. Cross builds take the
headers from the wasm CPython in the emscripten prefix instead, and link no
libpython at all -- a side module leaves the CPython symbols undefined for the
main module that embeds the interpreter to resolve at load time.

CppInterOp: the ExternalProject cannot drive the emscripten build, which needs
its own emcmake invocation and a natively-built cppinterop-tblgen to turn the
.td files into .inc. CPPJIT_USE_PREBUILT_CPPINTEROP points cppjit at an install
that already exists. That also makes the LLVM and Clang discovery unnecessary,
which is what lets the configure succeed at all here: the emscripten-forge
ClangConfig.cmake references lld targets, so it reports Clang as not found
unless LLD was found first, and libcppjit needs neither package -- it compiles
against CppInterOp's headers and links no LLVM or Clang library.

Target: emscripten's CMake platform module declares no shared-library support,
so a SHARED target would quietly have become static. The exception model has to
match the one CppInterOp and the hosting kernel were built with, or the tables
do not line up.

The two runtime paths baked into the library also stop being host paths. The
library name is now the one the kernel's main module already records its
dependency under, so dlopen hands back that same module rather than loading a
second copy of CppInterOp -- and with it a second interpreter. The include
directory is where the headers were preloaded into the browser's virtual
filesystem, not where they sit on the build machine.

(cherry picked from commit 1522e8d044236ba350d2b9b427cd710eb0345dc9)
